+\section{Three dimensions: standard rank-two hypergeometric structure and the remaining scalar problem}
+\label{app:d3RankTwo}
+
+\paragraph{Status of this revision.}
+This appendix identifies the residual functions in
+Appendix~\ref{app:threeDimensionalReduction} with \emph{standard real
+matrix-argument hypergeometric functions}.  The identification is exact
+and coefficientwise, not a numerical fit.  It does \emph{not} reduce them
+to ordinary one-variable ${}_pF_q$ values, and no new evaluation of the
+nonprimitive combination $\mathcal N_3$ is asserted.  Thus the
+ordinary-hypergeometric endpoint achieved for the two-dimensional
+primitive periods has not yet been achieved in three dimensions.
+The notation below makes a useful distinction: the residual objects are
+low-order functions on a rank-two symmetric cone, rather than arbitrary
+unclassified Horn arrays.  The earlier rational-node formulas remain the
+preferred explicit specification of the periods.
+
+\subsection{Normalization: the Jack parameter is not the spatial dimension}
+For a partition $\kappa=(m,n)$ with $m\geq n\geq0$, write
+\begin{equation}
+ [a]_\kappa=(a)_m(a-\tfrac12)_n,
+ \qquad |\kappa|=m+n.
+ \label{rt:partitionPochhammer}
+\end{equation}
+Throughout this appendix the Jack parameter is $\alpha=2$, the matrix
+size is two, and the physical dimension is $d=3$.  These three numbers
+refer to different notions.  Let $C_\kappa^{(2)}$ denote the
+$C$-normalized real zonal polynomial, so that
+$\sum_{|\kappa|=k}C_\kappa^{(2)}(X)=(\operatorname{tr}X)^k$.
+We use the standard definition \cite{MacdonaldHyperI,DLMFMatrixHyper}
+\begin{equation}
+ {}_pF_q^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}\boldsymbol a\\\boldsymbol b\end{matrix};zI_2\right)
+ =\sum_{m\geq n\geq0}
+ \frac{\prod_i[a_i]_{(m,n)}}{\prod_j[b_j]_{(m,n)}}
+ \frac{C_{(m,n)}^{(2)}(I_2)}{(m+n)!}\,z^{m+n}.
+ \label{rt:matrixDefinition}
+\end{equation}
+This definition fixes the conventional special function; it is not a
+claim that writing a summation as a function symbol has removed its
+rank-two structure.
+
+The identity that matches the angular multiplicity is
+\begin{equation}\boxed{
+ \frac{C_{(m,n)}^{(2)}(I_2)}{(m+n)!}
+ =\frac{2(m-n)+1}{(\tfrac32)_m n!}
+ =\frac{2(m-n)+1}{[\tfrac32]_{(m,n)}}.
+}\label{rt:hookWeight}\end{equation}
+Here is an explicit hook-product verification, including normalization.
+If $a(s)$ and $l(s)$ are the arm and leg lengths of a box of $\kappa$, the
+Jack hook product is
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ j_{(m,n)}(2)
+ &=\prod_{s\in(m,n)}(2a(s)+l(s)+1)(2a(s)+l(s)+2)\\
+ &=2^{2(m+n)}
+ \frac{(\tfrac32)_m\,m!\,n!\,(\tfrac12)_n}{2(m-n)+1}.
+\end{aligned}\label{rt:hooks}\end{equation}
+The specialization formula
+$C_\kappa^{(2)}(I_2)/|\kappa|!
+=2^{2|\kappa|}[1]_\kappa/j_\kappa(2)$ then gives
+\eqref{rt:hookWeight}.  In particular the factor $2\ell+1$ with
+$\ell=m-n$ is exactly the rank-two real-zonal weight after cancelling
+one $[3/2]_\kappa$.  This is why a real matrix-argument function appears.
+
+\subsection{A standard \texorpdfstring{${}_3F_2$}{3F2} of matrix argument for the tetrahedron}
+Retain the residual functions $\mathfrak H_r(t)$ of the preceding
+appendix and put
+\begin{equation}
+ a=t+\frac34,\qquad b=t+\frac32.
+ \label{rt:ab}
+\end{equation}
+For $r=3,4$ their exact identification is
+\begin{equation}\boxed{
+ \mathfrak H_r(t)=
+ {}_{r+1}F_r^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}a^{\times r},\ \frac32\\ b^{\times r}\end{matrix};I_2\right).
+}\label{rt:wheelMatrix}\end{equation}
+To verify it, use $m=\ell+n$ in the explicit Horn arrays.  The summand
+of either side is
+\begin{equation}
+ (2\ell+1)
+ \left[\frac{(a)_{\ell+n}(a-\frac12)_n}
+ {(b)_{\ell+n}(b-\frac12)_n}\right]^r.
+ \label{rt:wheelCoefficient}
+\end{equation}
+On the matrix side this follows from cancelling
+$[3/2]_{(m,n)}$ against \eqref{rt:hookWeight}.  Thus no summation theorem
+or exchange of a conditionally convergent sum is being assumed.
+The endpoint sums here are absolutely convergent: along $m\to\infty$
+with $n$ fixed the majorant is $m^{1-3r/4}$, and the remaining $n$
+factors are summable for $r=3,4$.
+
+At $t=0$ one upper parameter $3/2$ cancels a lower parameter.  The
+previous tetrahedron formula therefore becomes
+\begin{equation}\boxed{
+ Q_3=4\pi^2
+ {}_3F_2^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}\frac34,\frac34,\frac34\\\frac32,\frac32\end{matrix};I_2\right)
+ -\frac{\Gamma(\frac14)^{10}}{48\pi^{7/2}}.
+}\label{rt:Q3}\end{equation}
+Both remaining four-loop values likewise have short conventional arrays:
+\begin{equation}\boxed{\begin{aligned}
+ \mathfrak H_4(0)
+ &={}_4F_3^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}(\frac34)^{\times4}\\(\frac32)^{\times3}\end{matrix};I_2\right),\\
+ \mathfrak H_4(\tfrac18)
+ &={}_5F_4^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}(\frac78)^{\times4},\frac32\\(\frac{13}{8})^{\times4}\end{matrix};I_2\right).
+\end{aligned}}\label{rt:P4blocks}\end{equation}
+Substitution in the retained four-loop formula gives an equivalent
+matrix-argument presentation of $P_4(3)$ without changing its ordinary
+moment factors.  Neither of these matrix functions is to be evaluated
+by an ordinary scalar \texttt{hyper} call.
+
+A local coefficient check makes the distinction unambiguous.  With the
+same upper and lower parameters as in \eqref{rt:Q3},
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ {}_3F_2^{(\alpha=2)}(\tfrac34,\tfrac34,\tfrac34;
+                         \tfrac32,\tfrac32;zI_2)
+ &=1+\frac38z+\frac{2769}{12800}z^2
+        +\frac{15113}{102400}z^3+O(z^4),\\
+ {}_3F_2(\tfrac34,\tfrac34,\tfrac34;
+                         \tfrac32,\tfrac32;z)
+ &=1+\frac{3}{16}z+\frac{1029}{12800}z^2
+        +\frac{9317}{204800}z^3+O(z^4).
+\end{aligned}\label{rt:scalarDistinction}\end{equation}
+The first coefficients already differ.  This checks the normalization;
+it does not preclude an identity with some \emph{other} scalar
+hypergeometric functions or parameters.
+
+\subsection{Five loops: an exact three-term standard-function dictionary}
+The polynomial insertion in $\mathfrak H_7$ can also be expressed using
+ordinary parameter shifts of standard rank-two functions, rather than
+a bespoke extra Horn row.  Set
+\begin{equation}
+ v=t+\frac58,\qquad \delta_v=v(v-\tfrac12).
+ \label{rt:vdelta}
+\end{equation}
+Define three standard matrix-argument functions, all at $-I_2$:
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal J_A(t)&={}_9F_8^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}a^{\times7},\frac32,v+2\\b^{\times7},v\end{matrix};-I_2\right),\\
+ \mathcal J_B(t)&={}_{10}F_9^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}a^{\times7},\frac32,v+1,v+1\\b^{\times7},v,v\end{matrix};-I_2\right),\\
+ \mathcal J_C(t)&={}_9F_8^{(\alpha=2)}\!\left(
+ \begin{matrix}a^{\times7},\frac32,v+1\\b^{\times7},v\end{matrix};-I_2\right).
+\end{aligned}\label{rt:JABC}\end{equation}
+Then the exact dictionary is
+\begin{equation}\boxed{
+ \mathfrak H_7(t)=
+ \frac{\delta_{v+1}\mathcal J_A(t)
+       -\delta_v\mathcal J_B(t)-\mathcal J_C(t)}{2v-\frac12}.
+}\label{rt:H7matrix}\end{equation}
+The five arguments required by the retained real-node formula are
+$t=0,\pm1/16,3/16,5/16$.  They all have $v>1/2$, and all generalized
+Pochhammer factors in these arrays have positive real starting
+arguments.  At $t=0$, cancelling one common $3/2$ changes the three
+orders to ${}_8F_7$, ${}_9F_8$ and ${}_8F_7$, still \emph{of matrix
+argument}.  No coalescence limit or parameter differentiation is used.
+
+Here is the coefficientwise proof, which also fixes the sign of the
+matrix argument.  For $\kappa=(m,n)$ put
+$x=m+v$, $y=n+v-1/2$.  Direct Pochhammer cancellation gives
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ \frac{[v+1]_\kappa}{[v]_\kappa}&=\frac{xy}{\delta_v},\\
+ \frac{[v+2]_\kappa}{[v]_\kappa}
+ &=\frac{xy(x+1)(y+1)}{\delta_v\delta_{v+1}}.
+\end{aligned}\label{rt:insertionRatios}\end{equation}
+The only polynomial identity needed is
+\begin{equation}
+ xy(x+y)=xy(x+1)(y+1)-(xy)^2-xy.
+ \label{rt:insertionPolynomial}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently the right-hand side of \eqref{rt:H7matrix} multiplies the
+common coefficient \eqref{rt:wheelCoefficient}, with $r=7$, by
+$xy(x+y)/(\delta_v(2v-1/2))$, exactly the three polynomial ratios in the
+original $\mathfrak H_7$ array.  Finally
+$(-1)^\ell=(-1)^{m-n}=(-1)^{m+n}$, so the matrix argument is $-I_2$,
+\emph{not} $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$.
+Each of the three endpoint series separately converges absolutely.
+For example, the largest polynomial insertion has degree two in each
+of $m,n$; its absolute summand is bounded by a constant times
+$(m+1)^{-9/4}(n+1)^{-13/4}$ on $m\geq n$, which is summable.
+
+This dictionary standardizes the function class, but increases the
+number of displayed functions per Horn block from one to three.
+It is not advertised as a more economical numerical formula.  In
+particular, it does not eliminate the complementary ordered-lattice
+information isolated by the half-shift determinant.
+
+\subsection{A checked transformation that does not finish the tetrahedron}
+The standard matrix Kummer transformation is a useful diagnostic
+\cite[Eq.~(35.8.5)]{DLMFMatrixHyper}.  For
+$c_*=b_1+b_2-a_1-a_2-a_3$, it takes the parameter array to
+\begin{equation}
+ (a_1,a_2,a_3;b_1,b_2)
+ \longmapsto
+ (b_1-a_1,b_1-a_2,a_3;b_1,c_*+a_3)
+ \label{rt:kummerMap}
+\end{equation}
+with prefactor
+$\Gamma_2(b_2)\Gamma_2(c_*)/
+ [\Gamma_2(b_2-a_3)\Gamma_2(c_*+a_3)]$.
+For the array in \eqref{rt:Q3}, $c_*=3/4$; the transformed array is
+identical and the prefactor equals one.  Parameter permutations add
+nothing because the three upper parameters and two lower parameters
+are separately equal.  Therefore transformations generated by this
+map and these permutations do not expose a cancelling parameter or a
+terminating series.  This is a statement about this explicit route,
+not a proof that a different ordinary-${}_pF_q$ reduction cannot exist.
+
+The ordinary determinantal formulas for \emph{Hermitian}
+matrix-argument functions use Jack parameter $\alpha=1$, whereas
+\eqref{rt:hookWeight} fixes $\alpha=2$ here.  Transplanting the Hermitian
+formula would change the coefficients.  The distinction between real
+and Hermitian matrix-argument transformations is made explicitly in
+\cite{RichardsZhengMatrix}; it explains why the familiar determinant
+machinery is not itself the missing scalar identity.
+
+\subsection{Checks and the precise outstanding target}
+The companion script independently constructs the hook products and
+compares them with \eqref{rt:hookWeight}.  It then checks the wheel and
+five-loop parameter dictionaries on complete finite sets of partitions,
+including every rational sampling point required above.  It performs
+1107 exact checks, all passing.  Separate direct expansions of the
+matrix functions and of the Horn coefficients agree through total
+degree 90 at $z=1/4$ or $z=-1/4$ at 90 working decimal digits.  These
+are coefficient and interior-series checks, not interval bounds on
+endpoint master values.
+
+The retained primitive formulas were also freshly evaluated at 100
+working decimal digits, with radial cutoff 220 and asymptotic order
+80.  Their outputs begin
+\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
+ Q_3&=5.01710256455385536959085484569018369956\ldots,\\
+ P_4(3)&=62.05832794340667246646338858409673691827\ldots,\\
+ P_5(3)&=240.34031775908354981636654638800359825615\ldots.
+\end{aligned}\label{rt:numerical}\end{equation}
+This revision does not rerun the independent real-axis master integrals
+or the full graph calculation.  The preceding appendix retains those
+master-level comparisons and their evidence boundaries.
+
+A finite numerical reconnaissance examined 6479 convergent,
+noncancelling ordinary ${}_3F_2(1)$ and ${}_4F_3(1)$ arrays with upper
+parameters in $\{1/8,\ldots,1\}$ and lower parameters in
+$\{1/2,3/4,1,5/4,3/2\}$.  No integer relation involving the residual
+tetrahedron value, $1$, $\Gamma(1/4)^{10}/\pi^{11/2}$ and one of these
+values was returned at the recorded coefficient budget.  The exact
+settings and script are supplied; this bounded search is not an
+independence result and does not rule out other parameter arrays,
+arguments, prefactors or nonlinear identities.
+
+The smallest remaining primitive question can now be posed without a
+large array: evaluate the single rank-two ${}_3F_2$ in
+\eqref{rt:Q3} by a finite set of \emph{ordinary} scalar hypergeometric
+values.  The four- and five-loop complements require the related
+rank-two functions \eqref{rt:P4blocks} and \eqref{rt:H7matrix}.
+No such scalarization is proved here.  Moreover, reducing these
+primitive functions would still leave $\mathcal N_3$ for the complete
+thermal coefficient $C_5(3)$.  All original coefficient formulas,
+including their one- and two-dimensional specializations, are retained.
+
